<section id="single">
  <h2>Why the strict Opus single run scores 0 on the inventory suite</h2>
  <p>The inventory suite asks one answer to identify and solve 12 very different families, from 1-D Burgers to a 4-D elliptic problem. Every strict Opus answer was rejected before any code ran, and a rejected single run keeps the empty starting program, which fails every case:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>At the 64k-token output cap, all six answers (v1 and v2) were cut off while still writing.</li>
    <li>At Opus 5.5's own 128k maximum, the v1 answers finished, but their programs had 29,137–32,261 characters. All but one broke the 30,000-character limit that every configuration has. The one under the limit, and one over it, also used <code>import time</code>, which the sandbox bans.</li>
    <li>Under v2, one answer must write a separate part for every class. All three answers were cut off even at 128k.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>RSI and the native agents hit the same rules but recover by retrying. The single run with one rule repair gives the single run that one chance: it sends back only the rejection message, never a result. As a diagnostic outside the protocol, the three over-long v1 programs were also run exactly as written:</p>
  {{DIAGNOSTIC}}
  <p class="note">Run 2 also imports <code>time</code>, so it crashes when run, even with the length limit lifted. One answer can solve the whole suite, as run 1 shows, but not reliably.</p>
</section>

